exports[`renderAgentPrompt snapshots > renders the fresh board for the planner and the late board for the synthesizer 2`] = `
"Round 5 of 12.

# Problem (P1)
Should our four-person team move its three Node services into one pnpm monorepo, or keep separate repositories?

Constraints:
- No more than two weeks of migration effort
- CI must stay under 10 minutes

Success criteria:
- A recommendation that names the main risk
- Evidence cited for the effort estimate

# Human notes
- N1 (priority high): Prefer any option that keeps the three deploy pipelines independent.

# Entries in focus
## [hypothesis] H2 (target)
round 4, by solver, supersedes H1
parents: S2, E1, E2
confidence=0.75 supportedBy=E1,E2 contradictedBy=-
Consolidate into one pnpm workspace, migrating one service per sprint, but keep the three deploy pipelines separate and add task-level remote caching so CI only rebuilds what changed. This answers the CI-time critique and respects the human note.

## [synthesis] A1
round 4, by synthesizer
parents: H2, E1, E2
version=1 confidence=0.6 cites=H2,E1,E2
Recommendation: move to a single pnpm workspace one service per sprint, keeping the deploy pipelines independent and adding remote caching. Main risk: the effort estimate is unverified. Open: release cadence per service.

## [critique] C2
round 4, by critic
parents: H2
target=H2 severity=minor
The two-week effort figure is asserted, not estimated; a per-service breakdown would make it checkable.

## [evidence] E2
round 3, by researcher
parents: S2, H1
source=general knowledge supports=H1 contradicts=-
pnpm workspaces with task-level remote caching commonly keep CI under ten minutes for repositories of a few services, because unchanged packages are not rebuilt or retested.

## [evidence] E1
round 2, by researcher
parents: S1
source=repository audit, October 2026 supports=H1 contradicts=-
Two of the three services pin different TypeScript majors and duplicate 14 ESLint rules; dependency upgrades were applied to all three on separate dates in each of the last four months.

## [subproblem] S2
round 1, by planner
parents: P1
status=open
Estimate the migration effort and the CI-time impact of a pnpm workspace against the two-week and ten-minute constraints.

# Rest of the board
[subproblem] S4 r4 by solver: Inventory the shared tooling and dependency drift across the three services and say what duplication costs today.; status=done
[critique] C1 r3 by critic: No CI-time estimate: a single workspace that builds and tests all three services on every push may exceed the ten-minute constraint, and th…; target=H1 severity=major
[question] Q1 r1 by planner: Which of the three services releases most often, and does it share code with the others?; open

# Your targets
H2

Return JSON matching the schema. Reference entries only by the handles shown above (P problem, S subproblem, H hypothesis, E evidence, C critique, Q question, D decision, A synthesis, N human note), copied exactly."
`;

exports[`renderAgentPrompt snapshots > renders the mid-run board for the critic 1`] = `
"Round 4 of 12.

# Problem (P1)
Should our four-person team move its three Node services into one pnpm monorepo, or keep separate repositories?

Constraints:
- No more than two weeks of migration effort
- CI must stay under 10 minutes

Success criteria:
- A recommendation that names the main risk
- Evidence cited for the effort estimate

# Human notes
- N1 (priority high): Prefer any option that keeps the three deploy pipelines independent.

# Entries in focus
## [hypothesis] H1 (target)
round 2, by solver
parents: S1, S2
confidence=0.55 supportedBy=- contradictedBy=-
Consolidate the three services into one pnpm workspace with shared ESLint and TypeScript configuration, migrating one service per sprint. Shared tooling removes the drift, and the migration fits in two weeks if the deploy pipelines stay as they are.

## [evidence] E2
round 3, by researcher
parents: S2, H1
source=general knowledge supports=H1 contradicts=-
pnpm workspaces with task-level remote caching commonly keep CI under ten minutes for repositories of a few services, because unchanged packages are not rebuilt or retested.

## [critique] C1
round 3, by critic
parents: H1
target=H1 severity=major
No CI-time estimate: a single workspace that builds and tests all three services on every push may exceed the ten-minute constraint, and the hypothesis does not say how it avoids that.

## [subproblem] S3
round 2, by solver, supersedes S1
parents: P1
status=claimed
Inventory the shared tooling and dependency drift across the three services and say what duplication costs today.

## [evidence] E1
round 2, by researcher
parents: S1
source=repository audit, October 2026 supports=H1 contradicts=-
Two of the three services pin different TypeScript majors and duplicate 14 ESLint rules; dependency upgrades were applied to all three on separate dates in each of the last four months.

## [subproblem] S2
round 1, by planner
parents: P1
status=open
Estimate the migration effort and the CI-time impact of a pnpm workspace against the two-week and ten-minute constraints.

# Rest of the board
[question] Q1 r1 by planner: Which of the three services releases most often, and does it share code with the others?; open

# Your targets
H1

Return JSON matching the schema. Reference entries only by the handles shown above (P problem, S subproblem, H hypothesis, E evidence, C critique, Q question, D decision, A synthesis, N human note), copied exactly."
